   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.11",
    "topic_title": "Equivalent Representations",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = (x²−9)/(x−3), with domain consisting of all real inputs for which this expression is defined. Which rewritten formula and domain represent exactly the same function as f?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "f(x) = x+3, with domain all real x.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Lost-domain restriction: Canceling a factor does not make an originally undefined input valid. This representation allows x = 3, but the original expression has a zero denominator there. Fix: Record the original denominator’s restrictions before canceling factors."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "f(x) = x−3, with domain all real x such that x ≠ 3.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Wrong-factor cancellation: After factoring the numerator as (x−3)(x+3), canceling the denominator’s factor x−3 leaves x+3, not x−3. The domain matches, but the formula does not. Fix: Match the denominator’s factor to the identical numerator factor before canceling."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "f(x) = x+3, with domain all real x such that x ≠ 3 and x ≠ −3.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Excluding numerator zeros: A zero numerator does not by itself make a rational expression undefined. At x = −3, the original denominator is nonzero, so this restriction removes a valid input. Fix: Exclude only inputs that make the original denominator zero."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "f(x) = x+3, with domain all real x such that x ≠ 3.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Factoring gives x²−9 = (x−3)(x+3), so canceling x−3 yields x+3 wherever the original expression is defined. Keeping x ≠ 3 preserves the original domain, so both the outputs and allowed inputs match."
     }
    ]
   },
